---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Tokens">
  <h1>Tokens</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Design tokens are design decisions stored as named data. They are the smallest unit of the
    system, and the layer that makes theming, consistency, and cross-platform delivery possible.
  </p>

  <h2>The three tiers</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>1. Primitive (option) tokens</dt>
    <dd>
      Raw values with neutral names: <code>blue.600 = #1d4ed8</code>, <code>space.400 = 16px</code>.
      They describe what exists, not what it's for. Never referenced directly by components.
    </dd>
    <dt>2. Semantic (alias) tokens</dt>
    <dd>
      Purpose-named references to primitives: <code>color.text.primary → gray.900</code>,
      <code>color.border.danger → red.600</code>. This is the layer themes swap. Components should
      consume these.
    </dd>
    <dt>3. Component tokens (optional)</dt>
    <dd>
      Scoped overrides for one component: <code>button.primary.bg → color.action.primary</code>.
      Useful for fine-grained theming; skip the tier if it adds bureaucracy without a use case.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Naming</h2>
  <p>
    Most naming schemes follow some order of <em>category → concept → property → variant → state</em>
    (Salesforce's CTI taxonomy popularized this). The exact grammar matters less than: one grammar,
    applied everywhere, documented once. Test a proposed name by asking whether a new engineer could
    predict it without looking it up.
  </p>
  <pre><code>color.text.primary
color.text.on-accent
color.bg.surface.raised
color.border.danger
space.200            /* t-shirt or numeric scale: pick one */
font.size.heading-lg
motion.duration.fast</code></pre>

  <h2>The W3C community format</h2>
  <p>
    The Design Tokens Community Group (DTCG) defines a JSON interchange format so tools (Figma
    plugins, Style Dictionary, etc.) can share tokens. Core shape: <code>$value</code>,
    <code>$type</code>, optional <code>$description</code>, and <code>{'{group.token}'}</code>
    references for aliases:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "color": {
    "blue": {
      "600": { "$value": "#1d4ed8", "$type": "color" }
    },
    "text": {
      "link": {
        "$value": "{color.blue.600}",
        "$type": "color",
        "$description": "Default link text"
      }
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    A translation tool such as Style Dictionary turns this single source into CSS custom properties,
    iOS/Android values, and documentation.
  </p>

  <h2>Delivery on the web</h2>
  <p>
    CSS custom properties are the natural runtime target. Theme switching then becomes a scope
    change, not a rebuild:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`:root {
  --color-text-primary: #1e222b;   /* semantic layer */
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root { --color-text-primary: #e7e9ec; }
}

/* explicit user choice wins over OS preference */
[data-theme='dark'] { --color-text-primary: #e7e9ec; }`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Decisions to settle early</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Scale grammar:</strong> numeric (<code>space.100-900</code>) vs t-shirt (<code>sm/md/lg</code>). Numeric scales extend without renaming.</li>
    <li><strong>Units:</strong> <code>rem</code> for type and most spacing (respects user font-size settings); document the exceptions.</li>
    <li><strong>Who may add primitives:</strong> usually only the system team, otherwise the palette sprawls.</li>
    <li><strong>Contrast guarantees:</strong> encode which text/background pairings are AA-safe so themes can't silently break conformance.</li>
    <li><strong>Motion and elevation:</strong> tokenize them too, or they become the first sources of drift.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="sources">
    <h2>Primary reading</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://tr.designtokens.org/format/">DTCG Design Tokens Format Module (spec draft)</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.w3.org/community/design-tokens/">W3C Design Tokens Community Group</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Using_CSS_custom_properties">MDN: Using CSS custom properties</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://styledictionary.com/">Style Dictionary: token translation tooling</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
